
Value type vs Ref type. 

Aspect	Value Type	Reference Type
Memory storage	Stored directly on the stack	Stored on the heap, with a reference (pointer) on the stack
Data handling	Contains the actual data	Contains a reference to the data’s memory location
Assignment behavior	Copy of the value is created → changes don’t affect the original	Copy of the reference is created → changes affect the original object
Default value	Zero/null equivalent (e.g., 0 for int, false for bool)	Always null until explicitly assigned
Examples	int, float, bool, struct, enum	class, object, string, array, delegate
Performance	Faster, lightweight, suitable for small data	Slightly slower, better for complex objects and large data

"In C#, value types store the actual data directly on the stack, so when you assign one variable to another, a copy of the data is made. Examples include int, struct, and enum. On the other hand, reference types store a pointer to the data on the heap. Assigning one reference type variable to another copies the reference, not the data, meaning both variables point to the same object. Examples include class, string, and arrays. The key difference is that value types are copied by value, while reference types are copied by reference."

Ref vs Out, give real time example and usage. 

Aspect	Ref	Out
Initialization	Variable must be initialized before passing	Variable does not need to be initialized before passing
Purpose	Used when the called method needs to read and modify the value	Used when the called method needs to return multiple values
Direction	Two-way: data flows in and out	One-way: data flows out only
Usage scenario	When you want to update an existing value	When you want to return extra values without using return type
Example types	Updating a counter, modifying a flag	Parsing methods, returning multiple results


"In C#, both ref and out are used to pass arguments by reference. The difference is that ref requires the variable to be initialized before passing, and it allows both reading and writing inside the method. out does not require initialization, but the method must assign a value before returning. ref is useful when you want to modify an existing value, while out is typically used when you need to return multiple values from a method."





