"In C#, const defines a compile-time constant whose value cannot change and must be known at compile time. It is implicitly static. readonly, on the other hand, allows assignment at runtime, typically in a constructor, and ensures the value cannot be modified afterward. Use const for values like mathematical constants, and readonly for values that depend on runtime logic but should remain immutable once set."





What is garbage collector? 

"The garbage collector in .NET automatically manages memory by reclaiming unused objects from the heap. It works with generations to optimize performance and ensures developers don’t have to manually free memory. However, unmanaged resources must be explicitly released using IDisposable."
•  Memory allocation: When you create an object, memory is allocated from the managed heap.
•  Automatic collection: GC identifies objects no longer in use and reclaims their memory.
•  Generations: Objects are grouped into Gen 0, Gen 1, and Gen 2.
Gen 0: Short-lived objects (e.g., local variables).
Gen 1: Medium-lived objects.
Gen 2: Long-lived objects (e.g., static data).
•  Large Object Heap: Special area for objects > 85 KB, managed separately.
•  Finalization: Before reclaiming, GC may call finalizers (~Destructor) to clean up unmanaged resources.
•  Forced collection: Developers can trigger GC manually using GC.Collect(), though it’s generally discouraged.



How to remove unmanaged code?( How we remove garbage in unmanaged code?) 

•  Manual allocation: Memory is allocated using functions like malloc, calloc, or new.
•  Manual deallocation: Memory must be explicitly freed using free (C) or delete (C++).
•  Resource management: Handles, sockets, file descriptors, etc., must be closed manually.
•  RAII pattern: In C++, Resource Acquisition Is Initialization ensures resources are released when objects go out of scope (via destructors).
•  Smart pointers: Modern C++ uses unique_ptr, shared_ptr, and weak_ptr to automatically manage memory and reduce leaks.

"In unmanaged code, garbage is removed manually by the programmer using free or delete. Unlike .NET’s garbage collector, unmanaged environments rely on patterns like RAII and smart pointers to prevent leaks. The responsibility is entirely on the developer to ensure proper memory cleanup."

Throw vs Throw ex 

•  throw → Used when you want to bubble up the exception for logging or higher-level handling without losing context.
•  throw ex → Rarely used; only if you want to deliberately mask the original stack trace (e.g., security reasons).
"The difference between throw and throw ex is that throw preserves the original stack trace, while throw ex resets it. Best practice is to always use throw when re-throwing exceptions, because it makes debugging much easier."






