"Collections in C# are classes that store groups of objects. They come in non-generic, generic, concurrent, and specialized forms. In my projects, I’ve used List<T> for storing records, Dictionary<TKey, TValue> for fast lookups, and ConcurrentDictionary for thread-safe caching."


What is Dictionary, List, HashTable 
"A Dictionary is a generic, type-safe key-value collection, ideal for fast lookups. A List is a generic ordered collection, best for sequential data. A Hashtable is a non-generic key-value collection, mostly used in legacy code. In modern projects, we prefer Dictionary and List because they are type-safe and more efficient."

