What are delegates and built in delegates. 
In C#, delegates are objects that represent references to methods. They are essentially type-safe function pointers — allowing methods to be passed as parameters, stored, or invoked dynamically. Delegates are the foundation for events and callbacks in .NET.

"Delegates in C# are type-safe references to methods, enabling callbacks and event handling. Built-in delegates like Action, Func, and Predicate simplify common scenarios without needing custom delegate definitions. In my projects, I’ve used Func for reusable calculation logic and Predicate for filtering lists."

Example
// Define delegate
public delegate void PaymentHandler(decimal amount);

// Payment methods
public class PaymentGateway
{
    public void PayPalPayment(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Paid {amount} via PayPal");
    }

    public void StripePayment(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Paid {amount} via Stripe");
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        PaymentGateway gateway = new PaymentGateway();

        // Assign delegate dynamically
