Definition	Interface for in-memory iteration	Interface for remote query execution (e.g., DB)	Extension method that materializes results into a List<T>
Execution location	Client-side (in memory)	Server-side (translated into SQL or provider-specific query)	Client-side, after immediate execution
Execution timing	Deferred (runs when iterated)	Deferred (runs when iterated)	Immediate (runs at call time)
Performance	Loads all data, then filters in memory	Efficient — filters at source before fetching	May fetch all data at once, higher memory usage
Return type	IEnumerable<T>	IQueryable<T>	List<T>
Best use case	Working with collections already loaded in memory	Querying large datasets from databases or external sources	Caching results for reuse or multiple iterations
Flexibility	Limited to LINQ-to-Objects	Supports LINQ-to-SQL, LINQ-to-Entities, LINQ-to-Providers	Converts any query result into a concrete list
Deferred execution	Yes	Yes	No
Memory usage	Low (iterates on demand)	Low (fetches only needed data)	High (stores all results in memory)
Example scenario	Filtering a list of products already loaded from a file	Querying employees with salary > 50,000 directly from DB	Fetching employee list once and reusing it in UI rendering
Error handling	Errors occur during iteration	Errors occur during query translation/execution	Errors occur immediately at execution




What is yeild keyword? 
"The yield keyword in C# is used in iterator methods to return values one at a time (yield return) or stop iteration (yield break). It enables deferred execution and simplifies writing enumerators. In real projects, I’ve used yield for streaming large datasets and building custom collections without extra memory overhead."

Yield return → Provides the next element in a sequence without exiting the method.
Yield break → Ends the iteration immediately.
Deferred execution → The method’s state is preserved between iterations, so execution resumes where it left off.
Return types → Typically IEnumerable, IEnumerable<T>, IEnumerator, or IEnumerator<T>.

public static IEnumerable<int> EvenNumbers(int upto)
{
    for (int i = 0; i <= upto; i++)
    {
        if (i % 2 == 0)
            yield return i; // returns one value at a time
    }
}

// Usage
foreach (int num in EvenNumbers(10))
    Console.WriteLine(num); // Output: 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10

break vs continue vs return.
🔑 Break
Definition: Immediately exits the nearest enclosing loop (for, while, do-while, foreach) or switch block.
Effect: Stops the loop execution entirely.
🔑 Continue
Definition: Skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration of the loop.
Effect: Loop continues, but the remaining code in the current iteration is skipped.
🔑 Return
Definition: Exits the current method immediately and optionally returns a value.
Effect: Ends method execution, control goes back to the caller.


