What is TPL 
"TPL in C# is a high-level library for parallel and asynchronous programming. It uses tasks instead of manual threads, supports parallel loops, continuations, and cancellation. In real projects, I’ve used TPL for parallel data processing and handling multiple API calls efficiently."
Key Features of TPL
Task-based model → Represents asynchronous operations using Task and Task<TResult>.
Parallel loops → Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach for data parallelism.
Task continuations → Chain tasks with ContinueWith.
Cancellation → Cancel tasks gracefully using CancellationToken.
Exception handling → Aggregate exceptions from multiple tasks.
Efficient scheduling → Uses the .NET thread pool for optimal resource usage.


TPL vs Thread 
"Threads are low-level and give fine-grained control, but they’re harder to manage and scale. TPL is a higher-level abstraction that uses tasks, parallel loops, and thread pooling for efficient concurrency. In projects, I prefer TPL for most scenarios because it simplifies code and improves performance, but I use threads when I need direct control over execution."

Thread → When you need low-level control, e.g., setting thread priority or affinity.
TPL → For parallel data processing, handling multiple API calls, or background tasks in modern applications.
Aspect	Thread	TPL
Definition	Low-level unit of execution managed by the OS	High-level abstraction built on top of threads
Namespace	System.Threading	System.Threading.Tasks
Creation	new Thread(() => { ... })	Task.Run(() => { ... }) or Parallel.For
Management	Manual start, stop, join, sleep	Automatic scheduling via thread pool
Performance	Can waste resources if too many threads	Efficient, uses thread pool for reuse
Ease of use	Complex synchronization (locks, monitors)	Simplified with tasks, continuations, cancellation tokens
Scalability	Hard to scale for multi-core	Designed for multi-core scalability
Error handling	Exceptions must be caught manually	Exceptions aggregated into AggregateException
Use case	Fine-grained control over threads	Parallel loops, async tasks, high-level concurrency
		


Async vs Thread 
"Async is about non-blocking I/O and uses tasks with await, while Thread is about running code concurrently in separate execution paths. Async is best for I/O-bound operations, Thread for CPU-bound. In practice, I use async for web requests and database calls, and threads for heavy computations."

🔑 Async
Definition: A keyword used with methods to enable asynchronous programming.
Execution model: Non-blocking — allows the program to continue running while awaiting a task.
Underlying mechanism: Uses the Task Parallel Library (TPL) and the thread pool, but doesn’t necessarily create new threads.
Best for: I/O-bound operations (file access, web requests, database calls).
Syntax: async keyword with await to pause execution until a task completes.

🔑 Thread
Definition: A low-level unit of execution managed by the OS.
Execution model: Runs code concurrently in a separate execution path.
Underlying mechanism: Directly creates and manages OS threads.
Best for: CPU-bound operations (heavy calculations, simulations).
Syntax: new Thread(() => { ... })

Aspect	Async	Thread
Level	High-level (language feature)	Low-level (OS construct)
Use case	I/O-bound tasks (network, file, DB)	CPU-bound tasks (calculations, simulations)
Execution	Non-blocking, uses thread pool	Dedicated OS thread
Ease of use	Simplified with async/await	Manual management (start, join, abort)
Performance	Efficient for many concurrent I/O tasks	Can waste resources if too many threads
Error handling	Built-in with try/catch around await	Must handle manually
Scalability	Very high (thousands of async tasks)	Limited (too many threads cause overhead)

How to start a Task
"You can start a task in C# using Task.Run, Task.Factory.StartNew, or by creating a Task and calling Start. Task.Run is the most common and recommended approach for simplicity, while StartNew is used for advanced scenarios. Tasks can also return results using Task<T>."
Task.Run → Running background logging or sending emails asynchronously.
Task.Factory.StartNew → Scheduling tasks with cancellation tokens in enterprise apps.
Task<T> → Fetching data from APIs and returning results.

What is async and await?
🔑 Async
Definition: Marks a method as asynchronous.
Return types: Typically Task, Task<T>, or ValueTask.
Purpose: Allows the method to contain await expressions.
🔑 Await
Definition: Suspends execution of the method until the awaited task completes.
Non-blocking: The thread is freed to do other work while waiting.
Resumes execution: Once the task finishes, execution continues from the same point.

"Async marks a method as asynchronous, while await pauses execution until the task completes. Together, they make asynchronous code look synchronous, improving readability. In real projects, I use async/await for web requests, database calls, and file operations to keep applications responsive."






Difference between Async/Await and TPL. 
"Async/await is best for I/O-bound tasks because it keeps applications responsive by freeing threads while waiting. TPL is best for CPU-bound tasks because it efficiently distributes work across multiple cores. In practice, I use async/await for web requests and database calls, and TPL for parallel data processing or heavy computations."
Aspect	Async/Await	TPL
Definition	Language keywords (async, await) for asynchronous programming	A set of APIs (Task, Parallel) for parallel and concurrent programming
Primary Use Case	I/O-bound operations (web requests, DB calls, file I/O)	CPU-bound operations (data processing, simulations, parallel loops)
Execution Model	Non-blocking, frees thread while waiting	Uses thread pool to run tasks in parallel
Ease of Use	High-level, readable, looks like synchronous code	More manual, requires explicit task creation and management
Return Types	Task, Task<T>, ValueTask	Task, Task<T>
Error Handling	Standard try/catch around await	Exceptions aggregated into AggregateException
Performance	Scales well for thousands of async I/O tasks	Efficient for multi-core CPU utilization
Best Scenario	Keeping UI responsive during long-running I/O	Running heavy computations across cores


How does parallel processing work in .NET? 
"Parallel processing in .NET uses TPL and PLINQ to distribute work across multiple cores. TPL handles partitioning, scheduling, and exceptions, while PLINQ provides query-like syntax for parallelism. It’s best for CPU-bound workloads like data processing or simulations, but not for trivial loops where overhead outweighs benefits."

🔑 Core Components of Parallel Processing in .NET
Task Parallel Library (TPL)
Provides Task, Parallel.For, and Parallel.ForEach.
Handles partitioning, scheduling, cancellation, and exception aggregation automatically.
Dynamically scales concurrency to use all available processors.
Parallel LINQ (PLINQ)
Parallel implementation of LINQ-to-Objects.
Splits collections into partitions and processes them concurrently.
Ideal for query-like syntax with large datasets.
Thread-safe collections
Classes like ConcurrentDictionary, BlockingCollection, and ConcurrentQueue.
Ensure safe access to shared data across multiple threads.
Task schedulers
Control how tasks are queued and executed.
Default scheduler uses the .NET thread pool.






How does Task Parallel Library (TPL) work? 
"TPL works by representing work as tasks, scheduling them on the thread pool, and automatically partitioning workloads across CPU cores. It simplifies parallel loops, supports continuations, cancellation, and exception handling. In practice, I use TPL for CPU-bound workloads like data processing, while async/await is better for I/O-bound tasks."

🔑 How TPL Works
Task abstraction
A Task represents a unit of work (like a method or lambda).
Tasks can run asynchronously and return results (Task<T>).
Thread pool usage
TPL schedules tasks on the .NET thread pool, reusing threads instead of creating new ones.
This reduces overhead and improves scalability.
Parallel loops
Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach automatically partition work across multiple cores.
Task continuations
You can chain tasks using ContinueWith or await for sequential execution.
Cancellation and exceptions
Supports cooperative cancellation via CancellationToken.
Exceptions are aggregated into AggregateException for easier handling.
Load balancing
TPL dynamically balances workloads across available CPU cores.

Write code to call multiple APIs in parallel.
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient())
        {
            // Define multiple API calls
            Task<string> api1 = client.GetStringAsync("https://api.example.com/data1");
            Task<string> api2 = client.GetStringAsync("https://api.example.com/data2");
            Task<string> api3 = client.GetStringAsync("https://api.example.com/data3");

            // Run them in parallel
            await Task.WhenAll(api1, api2, api3);

            // Access results
            Console.WriteLine("API1 Result: " + api1.Result);
            Console.WriteLine("API2 Result: " + api2.Result);
            Console.WriteLine("API3 Result: " + api3.Result);
        }
    }
}






Difference between IAsyncEnumerable vs Task-based interface. 
🔑 Task-based Interface
Definition → Represents a single asynchronous operation.
Return types: Task (no result) or Task<T> (returns a result).
Execution model: Runs once, completes once, returns a single value or no value.
Best for: Asynchronous methods that produce one result (e.g., fetching data from an API).
🔑 IAsyncEnumerable<T>
Definition → Represents an asynchronous stream of data.
Return type: IAsyncEnumerable<T> (multiple values over time).
Execution model: Produces values one at a time asynchronously using await foreach.
Best for: Asynchronous methods that produce multiple results (e.g., streaming rows from a database).


How do you handle 10,000+ concurrent requests in Web API? 
🔑 Core Strategies
Async/Await everywhere
Use asynchronous controllers (async Task<IActionResult>) so threads aren’t blocked while waiting for I/O (DB, file, network).
This allows the thread pool to serve more requests concurrently.
Connection pooling
Reuse database and HTTP connections via pooling.
Prevents resource exhaustion when thousands of requests hit at once.
Load balancing
Distribute requests across multiple servers using Nginx, Azure Front Door, or AWS ELB.
Horizontal scaling ensures no single server is overwhelmed.
Caching
Use in-memory cache (MemoryCache), distributed cache (Redis), or response caching middleware.
Reduces repeated expensive operations.
Task Parallel Library (TPL)
For CPU-bound workloads, use Task.Run, Parallel.ForEach, or PLINQ to utilize multiple cores.
For I/O-bound workloads, rely on async/await instead of parallel loops.
Throttling & rate limiting
Protects backend services from overload.
Implement policies with ASP.NET Core middleware or API gateways.
Scalable hosting
Deploy on Kubernetes, Azure App Service, or AWS ECS with auto-scaling enabled.
Scale out dynamically based on traffic.

